      "thinking": "The real issue is that io-shop calls the pricing dependency with no timeout on the account page path, so a slow dependency stalls the whole render. Adding a timeout budget helps bound latency going forward, but since the response already carries took_ms, by the time we'd check a deadline the time is already spent \u2014 the fix needs to happen at the call site itself, not after the fact.\n\nI'm considering whether pricing is even essential to render: since it's just the basket total, maybe the right move is to bound the call with a timeout and degrade gracefully \u2014 render the page without the total rather than blocking, which means changing the seam to accept a timeout parameter and treating a timeout as a non-fatal fallback.\n\nSince the seam signature is likely fixed by callers/tests in the repo, I'm second-guessing whether adding an argument is safe versus enforcing a timeout policy internally in the pricing module using took_ms, though that only helps if we can actually bound the elapsed time rather than react after the fact. I'm now trying to find the minimal, defensible fix that fits how account_page invokes the seam and how the existing tests supply lambdas.\n\nI'm thinking through a cleaner approach: add a timeout constant greater than the slow-call threshold, and use a thread-based deadline inside basket_total. Rather than raising an error on timeout\u2014which would turn slowness into failure\u2014I want to degrade gracefully, making total_cents nullable and adding a timed_out flag that flows through to the rendered page and delay reporting.\n\nEven though the incident traced the fault to the dependency itself, io-shop's job is to survive that dependency being slow, so bounding the wait rather than waiting indefinitely is the right fix. I want to check test_account_page to see how the seam is constructed there so I don't break existing tests.\n\n",
